// A `/// @custom` body is opaque HLSL, so DreamShaderLang must have no opinion about the
// characters inside it. Every line of the body below would be a lexical error if this text were
// DreamShaderLang -- DSH2101 for a character that starts no token, DSH2106 for a `#` that is not
// the first thing on its line -- and none of it is reported, because the body is sliced out of
// the source and handed to the shader compiler (`dsc check --shaders` is what may complain).
//
// Braces are the one thing the capture does read, so they still have to balance.

/// @custom
/// @desc Characters that are not DreamShaderLang at all.
float3 HostileButOpaque(float2 UV)
{
    // A vendor intrinsic spelled with @, a $ register name, a ` and a mid-line #.
    float3 Result = $Globals.Tint @ UV.x # 2 `;
    int Marker = 'x';
    return Result;
}
